<page_footer><loc_286><loc_437><loc_298><loc_442>259</page_footer>
<page_break>
<page_header><loc_150><loc_30><loc_166><loc_38>260</page_header>
<page_header><loc_258><loc_31><loc_360><loc_37>J. ACZÉL AND C. WAGNER</page_header>
<text><loc_150><loc_45><loc_258><loc_53>follows from (4) and (5) that</text>
<formula><loc_200><loc_57><loc_416><loc_65>g ( u _ { 2 } , \cdots , u _ { n } ) + g ( v _ { 1 } , \cdots , v _ { n } ) - g ( u _ { 2 } + v _ { 2 } , \cdots , u _ { n } + v _ { n } ) = 0 ,</formula>
<text><loc_150><loc_69><loc_164><loc_76>i.e.,</text>
<formula><loc_150><loc_80><loc_410><loc_88>( 6 ) \quad g ( u _ { 2 } + v _ { 2 } , \cdots , u _ { n } + v _ { n } ) = g ( u _ { 2 } , \cdots , u _ { n } ) + g ( v _ { 2 } , \cdots , v _ { n } ) .</formula>
<text><loc_149><loc_92><loc_466><loc_108>By [2] and [1, pp. 215-16 and p. 32], the general solution of (6), continuous at a point or bounded from one side on an interval or on a set of positive measure, is</text>
<formula><loc_150><loc_112><loc_370><loc_120>( 7 ) \quad g ( u _ { 2 } , \cdots , u _ { n } ) = a _ { 2 } u _ { 2 } + \cdots + a _ { n } u _ { n } .</formula>
<text><loc_149><loc_124><loc_466><loc_140>Hence the general solution of (2) and (3), under these same weak regularity conditions, is, by (5),</text>
<formula><loc_150><loc_142><loc_413><loc_150>f ( x _ { 1 } , x _ { 2 } , \cdots , x _ { n } ) = ( 1 + a _ { 2 } + \cdots + a _ { n } ) x _ { 1 } - a _ { 2 } x _ { 2 } - \cdots - a _ { n } x _ { n }</formula>
<formula><loc_150><loc_152><loc_364><loc_160>= w _ { 1 } x _ { 1 } + w _ { 2 } x _ { 2 } + \cdots + w _ { n } x _ { n }</formula>
<text><loc_148><loc_166><loc_466><loc_181>with w1+w2+···+w.n = 1, as asserted. Note that, in (8), one or more of the numbers w.l may be negative.</text>
<text><loc_148><loc_181><loc_466><loc_212>Now if f is assumed to be an averaging function, the aforementioned boundedness conditions are clearly satisfied, and setting x.l = 1 and x.l = 0 for k 4 j yields 0.l.l = 1. Thus we have as a corollary to the above theorem the following characterization of weighted arithmetic means:</text>
<text><loc_148><loc_212><loc_465><loc_227>COROLLARY. Let f: R"->R. Then f is a weighted arithmetic mean if and only if f is an averaging function satisfying the k-allocation property for k = 2 and k = 3.</text>
<text><loc_147><loc_227><loc_465><loc_257>Remark 1. In the statement of the above corollary the averaging function condition (1) may be replaced by a considerably weaker supposition. It is clearly sufficient, for example, that there exist some c>0 (no matter how small) such that 0.l.l = c (j = 1, 2, ···, n) implies 0.l.l(x, x2, ···, x).</text>
<text><loc_147><loc_257><loc_465><loc_280>Remark 2. The foregoing theorems arose in connection with a study of arithmetic averaging as a method of amalgamating a set of individual opinions as to the most appropriate values of some sequence of decision variables. (See [3].)</text>
<text><loc_146><loc_280><loc_465><loc_341>Suppose that there are k decision variables and n individuals and we denote by x.l the opinion of individual j as to the most appropriate value of variable i. In many of these problems (such as the allocation of a fixed sum of money among k competing projects) the column sums of the matrix (x.l) are required to have a common value s. If a group adopts as the consensual value of variable i the weighted arithmetic average x.l = W1.l1 + W2.l2 +···+ W.l.l the consensual values have the highly desirable property x.l + x2+···+x.k = 5. The above results assert that weighted arithmetic means are the only averaging functions with this property.</text>
